  m_Name: TensionCompression
  m_EditorClassIdentifier:
  lessonId: civil-craft-tension-compression-lesson-v1
  lessonTitle: Tension & Compression
  lessonImage: {fileID: 0}
  popupDescription: "<b><#E09500>Tension</color></b> is a pulling force that causes a structural member to stretch. <b><#E09500>Compression</color></b> is a pushing force that causes a member to shorten or become compressed.\n\nKey points:\n- Tension pulls structural members apart.\n- Compression pushes structural members together.\n- Different members can experience different forces under the same load.\n- Members must be strong enough to withstand the forces acting on them.\n\nThis lesson has been added to your <b><#E09500>Almanac</color></b>."
  almanacImage: {fileID: 0}
  lessonDescription: "<b><#E09500>Tension</color></b> is a pulling force that stretches a structural member. Forces pull away from each other at opposite ends of the member. Excessive tension can cause the member to stretch too much or break.\n\n<b><#E09500>Compression</color></b> is a pushing force that squeezes or shortens a member. Forces push toward each other from opposite ends. Excessive compression can cause crushing or buckling, depending on the material and shape.\n\nWhen a vehicle crosses a bridge, the roadway and connected structural members transfer its weight toward the supports. In a <b><#E09500>truss bridge</color></b>, some members can carry tension while others carry compression under the same load. The forces in each member depend on the structural arrangement, support positions, and where the vehicle is along the crossing.\n\nEngineers must choose suitable materials and arrange members so they can safely withstand these forces. Understanding tension and compression helps identify which parts need stronger members or a better load path; it does not remove the need to test the bridge.\n\nFor <b><#E09500>Vance's transport crossing</color></b>, load the cargo onto the vehicle before testing. The cargo adds to the vehicle's load. Watch how the members work together as the loaded vehicle crosses, and check how their responses change as it moves."
